<NoteLayout
  title="Building a Reporting Stack from Scratch"
  description="From raw event data to executive dashboard: a practical guide to building a reporting stack with GA4, BigQuery, and Looker Studio."
  articleSlug="building-a-reporting-stack"
  schema={schema}
>
  <p class="description scribble-target">Most teams inherit a reporting setup they didn't design. Dashboards accumulate, definitions drift, and the number that marketing calls "sessions" differs from what product calls "visits." Starting from first principles fixes this permanently.</p>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Layer 1 — Event Collection</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Everything downstream depends on clean data arriving at the collection layer. Before building dashboards, audit what you are collecting and how.</p>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Implement GA4 via Google Tag Manager. Keep all tracking configuration in GTM — not hardcoded in the site — so changes don't require deploys.</li>
    <li>Define a measurement plan before instrumentation. Document every custom event, the parameters it carries, and what business question it answers.</li>
    <li>Use consistent naming: snake_case for event names, explicit parameter names. Rename automatically collected events only if your naming convention conflicts.</li>
    <li>Enable the BigQuery export immediately. Even if you don't use it today, you cannot backfill raw event data retroactively.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Layer 2 — Data Warehouse (BigQuery)</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>GA4 exports to BigQuery daily and in near-real-time (streaming). The daily export is sufficient for most reporting use cases.</li>
    <li>Flatten the events table into analysis-ready views. The raw schema is deeply nested — a sessions view, a conversions view, and a user-level view cover 90% of needs.</li>
    <li>Apply transformations in dbt or BigQuery scheduled queries. Keep raw exports untouched; all business logic lives in the transformation layer.</li>
    <li>Document each view in a data dictionary. Column names, definitions, and any filters applied. This prevents the definition drift that makes dashboards untrustworthy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Layer 3 — Visualisation (Looker Studio)</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Connect Looker Studio directly to BigQuery views, not to the raw GA4 connector. This gives you control over the underlying data model.</li>
    <li>Design for the audience. Executive dashboards need three to five KPIs with clear trend context. Analyst dashboards need drill-down capability and filters.</li>
    <li>Set a single source of truth for each metric. If revenue appears in three dashboards with three different numbers, trust in all three collapses.</li>
    <li>Schedule automated email delivery for key stakeholder reports. Dashboards that require login are rarely checked.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Maintenance and Governance</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Assign a data owner for each domain — someone accountable when a metric breaks or a definition changes.</li>
    <li>Run a quarterly data audit: check event volumes, validate conversion counts against source-of-truth systems, review unused custom dimensions.</li>
    <li>Version-control your dbt models and BigQuery views in a Git repository. Reporting logic that breaks silently is more dangerous than code that breaks loudly.</li>
  </ul>
</NoteLayout>
